      "documentSlug": "supplements-coq10-creatine-vitamin-d-mucuna-and-failed-neuroprotectants",
      "documentConcept": "Supplements: CoQ10, creatine, vitamin D, Mucuna and failed neuroprotectants",
      "canonicalConceptId": "concept:a4d65f098a7e7c29ae1cf6455b065f19a93197613d930b9e9b8abd3e363ecee0",
      "text": "Trial evidence for supplements and failed neuroprotectants in Parkinson disease is largely negative in the sources read. Large trials of coenzyme Q10 (QE3), creatine (NET-PD LS-1), inosine (SURE-PD3) and isradipine (STEADY-PD III) each reported no clinical benefit, as did a tocopherol trial. Earlier signals, a CoQ10 dosage-ranging trial reporting a positive trend and a creatine futility screen that did not reject it, were followed by later large trials reporting no benefit. Vitamin D reports are mixed. Mucuna pruriens trials are small and short, and commercial products varied widely from labelled levodopa. NICE NG71 recommends against vitamin E and, except in the context of clinical trials, against co-enzyme Q10 as neuroprotective therapy, recommends that creatine supplements not be offered, and advises a vitamin D supplement. This summarizes what sources report; it is not medical advice.",
